{
  "dom_id": "step-q32976",
  "name": "Question 7",
  "problem": "Calculate ${lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖$, if $f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\,.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, recall that:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} \\\\ {lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the limits one-by-one.\n\n- For the first component, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{s}{(s+1)^{2}} & =\\frac{0}{(0+1)^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The second component can be found using a special limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡s}{{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={({lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡s}{s})}^{2} \\\\  & ={1}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The third component can be found using L'Hopital's Rule twice:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{2{s}^{2}} & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡s)}^{″}}{{(2{s}^{2})}^{″}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{s\\to 0}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡s}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]\\,.$\n\nFinally, the limit of $‖f(s)‖$ as $s$ approaches $0$ is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{s\\to 0}‖f(s)‖ & =‖{lim}_{s\\to 0}f(s)‖ \\\\  & =‖[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1/4\\end{aligned}]‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{17}{16}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
